Skip a scanout flush into a dma-buf the compositor still holds.

Ika's compositor (ika_stream) holds the buffer of a dma-buf frame until the
console has released the frame. Flipping the scanout to a buffer still held
would show the guest's next composition in the frame on screen, so the flush
is dropped instead, as the copy path already drops a frame whose buffer the
compositor has not released: the guest's next flush, into another buffer of
its swapchain, gets through. The buffer on screen is exempt (see
dwl_surface_dmabuf_in_use), so a single-buffer scanout keeps updating.

diff --git a/devices/src/virtio/gpu/virtio_gpu.rs b/devices/src/virtio/gpu/virtio_gpu.rs
--- a/devices/src/virtio/gpu/virtio_gpu.rs
+++ b/devices/src/virtio/gpu/virtio_gpu.rs
@@ -413,6 +413,12 @@ impl VirtioGpuScanout {
         if let Some(import_id) =
             VirtioGpuScanout::import_resource_to_display(display, surface_id, resource, rutabaga)
         {
+            // Ika: the compositor holds the buffer of a frame while the console shows it. Drop
+            // this flush rather than show the guest drawing into that frame; the next flush
+            // comes in another buffer.
+            if display.borrow().import_in_use(surface_id, import_id) {
+                return Ok(OkNoData);
+            }
             display
                 .borrow_mut()
                 .flip_to(surface_id, import_id, None, None, None)
